13:10:23 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a genuinely important question, and it deserves a real answer rather than another round of brain science. ## The short answer: this isn't really about trust, it's about resolution School science education (K-12, intro college courses) is designed to give you a **working model**, not a complete, nuanced account of current research. This is true across nearly every subject: - **Physics**: You learn Newtonian mechanics first, even though it's technically "wrong" at very small scales or near light speed (quantum mechanics/relativity). Newton's model isn't a lie—it's a simplified, extremely useful approximation for everyday purposes. - **Biology**: "Junk DNA" was taught for decades as non-functional, then research revealed much of it has regulatory functions. Not a lie—an incomplete picture at the time. - **Nutrition**: The food pyramid, fat vs. carbs advice, egg/cholesterol warnings—all revised multiple times as research evolved. - **History**: Simplified national narratives get complicated once you study primary sources and multiple perspectives in college-level work. This isn't evidence that school failed you—it's how teaching complex, evolving fields to beginners *necessarily* works. You can't teach a 16-year-old the full nuance of neuroscience research any more than you can teach quantum field theory to someone who hasn't learned algebra yet. ## What you actually learned that still holds up - The brain does undergo *dramatic* change in youth, more than adulthood - The prefrontal cortex *does* mature later than other regions - Judgment/impulse control *does* generally improve with age into the mid-20s - Age 25 *is* a real, if fuzzy, marker of a genuine developmental transition None of that was false. What was oversimplified was the *hard stop* framing—the idea that development is binary (happening/not happening) rather than continuous and shifting in nature. ## The actual skill this teaches you Rather than "distrust everything," the